==============================================================================
 XFMGR2 - an XTree-style file manager for the Commander X16    (Build 195)
==============================================================================

 A dual-pane, keyboard-driven file manager. A collapsible DIRECTORY tree on
 the left, the selected directory's FILES on the right, with file tagging, a
 banked text/hex viewer with BASIC/Markdown syntax coloring, a BMX image
 viewer, a whole-disk Find, and a full set of file operations - copy, move,
 rename, delete, mkdir and prune - plus edit via the ROM X16 Edit and
 switchable color themes.

 The bottom command menu is MODIFIER-DRIVEN. By default it shows MENU (plain
 keys). Hold CTRL for the CTRL menu, hold ALT (the Commodore key) for the ALT
 menu; release to return to MENU. The plain menu is context-sensitive: the
 DIRECTORY pane and the FILE pane offer different commands.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 NAVIGATION (either pane)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Up / Dn     Move the cursor within the focused pane
   TAB         Switch focus between the DIRECTORY and FILE panes
   Right       Focus the FILE pane (logs the dir's files on the fly).
               On a folder that holds no files, expands it instead -
               there is nothing to step sideways into, so Right does
               the other thing it means in a tree.
   Left        Focus the DIRECTORY pane
   F1          Show this help
   Q           Quit, leaving the shell in the LAUNCH directory

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 DIRECTORY pane (plain keys)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Enter       Log the directory if new; else expand / collapse it
   M           Make a new subdirectory in the selected folder
   R           Rename the selected directory
   D           Delete the selected folder (empty folders only)
   S           Showall - list every logged file on the DISK as one flat
               file window (see SCOPED FILE VIEWS below)
   B           Branch - the same, but only this folder and everything
               logged below it
   G           Global - in XTree this widens Showall from the current
               disk to EVERY logged disk. XFMGR drives one volume, so
               there is nothing further to widen to and G does exactly
               what S does. It is here so the menu matches XTree's.
   A           About box (version, banked-RAM usage, credits)

   (Prune and Re-log are ALT commands - Alt-P and Alt-F3 - see the ALT menu.)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 FILE pane (plain keys)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   T           Tag current file, then advance
   U           Untag current file, then advance
   V           View - .bmx in the image viewer, else the text/hex viewer
   P           Play music - .zsm (ZSound) or .wav (PCM) audio
   E           Edit file in the ROM X16 Edit, then return
   C           Copy selected (or all tagged) file(s) to a directory
   M           Move selected (or all tagged) file(s) to a directory
   R           Rename (supports * and ? wildcards)
   D           Delete current file (confirm)
   F           Set the file-spec filter (e.g. *.prg, Enter = * = all)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 SCOPED FILE VIEWS - Showall (S), Branch (B) and Global (G)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 From the DIRECTORY pane, these widen the file pane to the whole screen
 and fill it from more than one folder:

   S  Showall   every logged file on the disk, as one flat list
   B  Branch    the selected folder and everything logged below it
   G  Global    every logged DISK - identical to S on a single-drive
                machine, which is what the X16 is here

 It is the SAME file window with the SAME commands, not a separate screen.
 Tag, Untag, View, Play, Edit, Copy, Move, Rename, Delete and the Ctrl
 commands all work exactly as they do in a normal listing - each file is
 acted on IN ITS OWN FOLDER, so Ctrl-C and Ctrl-M gather tagged files from
 all over the disk into one destination, and Ctrl-S searches their contents
 wherever they live.

 Because the rows come from many folders, the Path line at the top follows
 the HIGHLIGHTED FILE rather than the tree cursor - it is the only thing
 telling you which folder the row under the bar belongs to. The title bar
 shows the scope and the active filespec; the counts on the right are for
 the whole list.

 Only files in LOGGED directories appear - log more of the tree (Enter on
 a folder) to widen what Showall can see. The FileSpec (F) still applies,
 so "F *.prg" then S lists every program on the disk. The list holds up to
 2048 files; past that the title shows (partial) and the count reads
 "2048 of N".

 Esc (or TAB back to the tree) returns to the normal two-pane view.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 CTRL menu (acts on the current directory / all tagged)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Ctrl-T      Tag all files in this directory
   Ctrl-U      Untag all files in this directory
   Ctrl-I      Invert tags in this directory
   Ctrl-W      Tag files matching a wildcard
   Ctrl-V      View every TAGGED file in turn, starting at the first.
               In the viewer, + moves to the next tagged file and -
               back to the previous (paging past the end also moves
               on); the footer shows "File n/m" so you can see where
               you are. Esc/Q ends the walk on the file you are on.
               If you ran a Ctrl-S search first, each file opens ON
               its first hit with the text highlighted. N or SPACE
               then walks the hits, and once a file's hits run out it
               rolls straight on to the next file - so you can read
               every occurrence across the whole set with one key.
               (Ctrl-L in the emulator, Ctrl-V on hardware)
   Ctrl-S      Search the TAGGED files for text; the ones that do NOT
               contain it are untagged, so the tags collapse to the hits
               (Ctrl-E in the emulator, Ctrl-S on hardware)
   Ctrl-F      Find files across the whole disk
               (Ctrl-N in the emulator, Ctrl-F on hardware)
   Ctrl-C      Copy all tagged files (from all dirs) to one destination
   Ctrl-M      Move all tagged files (from all dirs) to one destination
               (Ctrl-O in the emulator, Ctrl-M on hardware)
   Ctrl-X      Delete all tagged files in this directory
               (Ctrl-X in the emulator, Ctrl-D on hardware)

   The XTree working loop: tag the files you care about (T one at a time,
   or Ctrl-T for all), narrow them with Ctrl-S until only the interesting
   ones are still tagged, then Ctrl-V to read through exactly those.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ALT menu  (ALT = the Commodore key)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Alt-J       Jump to dir - type a path and go there, logging every
               level on the way down (XTree's Treespec). Works for a
               folder that has never been logged, which is how you widen
               what Showall and Branch can see. F2 picks from the tree.
   Alt-R       Release - un-log the current folder to free its memory
   Alt-T       Tag branch - tag every logged file in this folder AND
               everything below it, without entering the Branch view.
               Honours the FileSpec, so "F *.prg" then Alt-T tags every
               program in the branch.
   Alt-U       Untag all - clear every tag on the DISK. Ctrl-U only
               reaches the current listing; after a Showall session the
               tags are spread over folders you are no longer looking
               at, and this is how you start clean. Ignores the FileSpec
               and the 1024-row list cap on purpose - a tag you cannot
               see is still a tag.
   Alt-S       Pick sort order: name -> extension -> size. Each tap moves
               the highlighted order in the ALT menu; the list is re-sorted
               once, when you let ALT back up.
   Alt-X       Execute - quit XFMGR and chain-run the selected program
   Alt-Q       Quit, leaving the shell in the CURRENTLY SELECTED directory
   Alt-F3      Re-log the current context (sub-folders in DIR, files in FILE)
   Alt-P       Prune - recursively delete the selected folder and everything
               under it (DIR pane; type 'prune' to confirm)
   Alt-F10     Open the color-theme setup (quits to XFSETUP.PRG)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 In text prompts (Copy, Move, Rename, Mkdir, Filespec, Tag-spec, Find)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Up          Pop up the recent-entry history for this prompt
   F2          Open the directory picker (Copy/Move destinations)
   Left/Right  Move the cursor in the field   (Home = start)
   Enter       Accept (saved to history)
   Esc         Cancel

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 In the Find / directory-picker modals
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Up / Dn     Move          Enter  Select / jump to the file
   +           Expand (picker, logs on demand)     - Collapse
   Esc / Q     Cancel

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 File formats the viewer handles
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Text        ANY file opens as text. Non-printable bytes show as '.'.
               Press H at any time for a raw hex dump, H again for text.
   Hex         Any file, as a hex + ASCII dump (H toggles text <-> hex).
   ZSM music   .zsm files (auto-detected) show a parsed header breakout -
               version, tick rate, loop point, PCM, FM (YM) and PSG voices.
               Press H for the raw hex bytes.
   BMX image   .bmx bitmaps do NOT open here - V sends them full-screen to
               the BMX image viewer; any key returns to the file list.

   Big files that are too large for the pager fall back to the ROM X16 Edit.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Syntax coloring in the viewer (C)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Auto        BASIC / BASLOAD source (.bas, .bas.txt, .basl, .bl) and
               Markdown (.md) are colored automatically when opened.
   BASIC       Statement keywords, built-in functions, "strings", numbers,
               and REM / ## comments each get their own color. A dotted
               BASLOAD label (DIR.READ.BIN) stays plain - it is one name.
   Markdown    A leading # is a heading, ## or deeper a subheading.
   C           Toggles coloring on / off. Switching back on uses the mode
               for the file's extension, or BASIC if the extension was not
               recognised - so C also colors a listing saved under any name.
   Needs       xsyntax.ovl beside the program; without it the viewer simply
               shows plain text and the C key is not offered.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Music playback (P)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ZSM         .zsm songs play through the zsmkit engine (FM + PSG + PCM).
               The whole song is loaded into free RAM banks, so a very large
               song may report "no free RAM banks" - close/relog to free some.
               (V on a .zsm still shows the header breakout; P plays it.)
   WAV         .wav plays uncompressed PCM: 8- or 16-bit, mono or stereo, up
               to ~48 kHz. Compressed (ADPCM) wavs are not supported yet.
   Controls    SPACE pauses / resumes; Q, ESC or STOP stops and returns.
   Needs       zsmkit.bin (ZSM) and xmusic.ovl (WAV) beside the program; if
               either is missing, P reports it and does nothing.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 In the text / hex viewer (V, and this help)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   PgDn/PgUp   Page          T  Top        B  Bottom
   H           Toggle hex / text
   F           Find a string (always searches from the top)
   N / Space   Repeat the search. At end-of-file it wraps back to the top
               and reports "wrapped to top", so a search cycles round.
   C           Toggle syntax coloring on / off (see below)
   I           Read the file as ASCII/ISO or as PETSCII. The viewer
               already GUESSES this from the file's own bytes when it
               opens - text saved through the KERNAL (BASLOAD sources,
               MSEDIT buffers, .SEQ files) is PETSCII, and read as ASCII
               every letter of it shows as '.'. The footer says which
               reading you are in: ISO or I PET. This key overrides the
               guess; glyphs only - nothing is re-encoded, and the file
               is never written.
   W           Cycle how long lines are laid out:
                 Wrap off   one file line = one screen row, cut at the
                            right edge; Left/Right pan across it. The
                            DEFAULT, because it is the only mode that
                            does not invent line breaks the file has not
                            got - a wrapped line reads as two.
                 Wrap char  break anywhere, so every byte is on screen
                 Wrap word  break at spaces, for prose
               Changing this keeps your place, but PgUp cannot go back
               past the point where you changed it.
               Syntax coloring switches off while a line is panned
               sideways, and on word-wrapped lines wider than the
               screen: in both cases the colors could not be put on the
               right characters, so they are left off rather than wrong.
   Q / Esc     Exit back to the file list

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 EMULATOR vs REAL HARDWARE  (key differences)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   XFMGR detects whether it is running under the x16emu emulator and rebinds
   the CTRL commands the emulator window grabs before they reach the app. The
   bottom CTRL menu always shows the binding that is ACTIVE right now.

     Command          Emulator     Real hardware
     Delete tagged    Ctrl-X       Ctrl-D
     Find files       Ctrl-N       Ctrl-F
     Move tagged      Ctrl-O       Ctrl-M
     Search tagged    Ctrl-E       Ctrl-S
     View tagged      Ctrl-L       Ctrl-V

   The emulator also swallows Ctrl-S, so Tag-by-wildcard is Ctrl-W in both
   environments. ALT is the Commodore (C=) key on a real X16; in the
   emulator it is the left Alt key. Everything else - plain keys, function
   keys, arrows - behaves identically in both.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 LIMITS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Directories Up to 254 directories are logged per session (the tree caps
               there). Find scans the WHOLE disk no matter how many folders it
               holds, but only logs the folders that CONTAIN a match. If one
               search's matches are spread across more than 254 folders,
               logging stops there and the results are marked "(partial)".
   Find/ShowAll At most 2048 matching files are collected; beyond that the list
               is marked "(partial)". Very deep folder paths may be skipped
               (also shown as "(partial)").

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 HOW IT IS INSTALLED  (folder layout)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The installer keeps the whole app in ONE folder and drops a tiny launcher
   in the drive root, so you can start it from anywhere:

     /                     drive root
     +-- XT                one-line BASIC launcher  (LOAD "/XFMGR/XFMGR.PRG")
     +-- XFMGR/            everything else lives here
          +-- XFMGR.PRG        the main program
          +-- *.OVL            banked overlays (viewer, image, music, ui, misc)
          +-- XFSETUP.PRG      the color-theme setup (Alt-F10)
          +-- ZSMKIT.BIN       the ZSM music engine
          +-- XFMGR.HLP        this help file
          +-- XFMGR.CFG        your saved theme (kept across updates)

   To install: Copy the unpacked release files plus INSTALL.PRG into one
   folder, boot the X16, CD into that folder and run INSTALL. It creates
   /XFMGR, copies the app files into it, and writes the root /XT launcher.
   (The install folder can be removed after the install is complete)

   To launch from *any* directory afterwards:   ^/xt
   (XFMGR itself always presents the tree rooted at "/", so you can roam the
   whole drive no matter where you started it.)

